s something  has always existed well it's an inner  calling and I believe in what my dance  symbolizes that is a fertility and its  significance I think if girls get you  know part of me the ritual actually  begins with the selection of the pole  the dancers go out in search of a tall  and sturdy tree in the nearby forests  que nosotros hacemos des vestidos de  blanco dress all in white that's how we  present ourselves to nature when we take  the tree from her in return we give her  incense cigars holy water spirits  flowers and fruits de is what in the--  we go to the tree and bring these gifts  one after another gracias a los por ESO  el sabado todo lo que seamos we cross  ourselves and prostrate ourselves when  we hand them over then we cut the tree  down everyone after the tree is felled  it's taken to the village and set up in  the main square offerings are then made  to the gods among them a live turkey if  your holiday we will we take this live  turkey and put it into the hole that  anchors the tree so the trunk actually  kills it doses give us a that grants us  protection no para nosotros the  tradition has been handed down from  father to son through the ages surviving  even mexico's colonial era the dance is  now gaining in popularity although  partly for its value is  practical the town of Quetta and El  Progreso itself still has many  colonial-era buildings along with a  network of cobbled streets many  residents go about in indigenous dress  giving visitors a sense of what life was  like here centuries ago quit salon is  home to the only statue of the Virgin  Mary in the whole of Mexico that wears  indigenous dress she's worn this white  robe embroidered with simple but  colorful designs for seven years now a  solicitor we asked the Vatican for  permission to dress the Virgin Mary in  this costume typical of kawat salon we  sent in photos and fortunately they  agreed las fotos de como el traje tipico  is we identify ourselves with her we say  this is our Blessed Virgin our mother of  God and she dresses like we do for  people here the statue is also a way to  teach younger generations about their  heritage school is also instrumental  lessons here are taught in two languages  in Spanish and Nava to the ancient  language of the Aztecs quit salon del  progreso one small but fascinating patch  in Mexico's multicultural quilt of more  than 60 indigenous groups  and that's all for this week's edition  of worlds stories for all of us here in  our partner stations and contributing  reporters around the world thanks for  watching don't forget you can watch the  show again at our website that's DWD  forward slash world stories before we go  here are some images that caught our eye  this week in our pics of the world see  you next time for more world stories
 

